  {
   "type": "text",
   "id": "annot-merge-20260731-9a3f1c07",
   "x": 70,
   "y": 7830,
   "width": 916,
   "height": 280,
   "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-31 (merge of the capability-contract and Cofre lines): three modules that had\nshipped WITHOUT a place on this map now have one, and the tier table names them. api/src/memvault/\nsits at tier 3 beside knowledge/ (imports data/ + config only). api/src/security/ is 2b - THE\nvalue-keyed redaction module plus origin-binding and path-containment; it imports services/ and is\nconsumed by cofre/, bridge/, integrations/ and automation/. api/src/cofre/ is 2c - credential\ncustody whose raw item/grant stores are lint-banned outside the module, so every read goes through\nunwrap(); it imports data/ + security/ and is consumed by bridge/, automation/ and routes/. They are\nnumbered 2b/2c rather than renumbering the table because they genuinely sit between the existing\ntiers 2 and 3, and every other document's tier reference stays valid.\n\nThe merge also moved ONE edge on this map: automation/service.ts no longer re-derives the approval\nscope for a local_command consent. executors/local-command.ts records the owner+org+MACHINE scope it\nlooked the approval up under onto the run's ConsentRequest, and resolveConsent banks the answer in\nthat scope. Before this the executor read under the connected daemon's real pairingId while the\nresolver wrote under null, so \"aprovar sempre\" stored a row the lookup could never read and the\nprompt returned forever. The party that will RE-READ the key is now the party that decides it.",
   "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-31 (merge of the capability-contract and Cofre lines): three modules that had\nshipped WITHOUT a place on this map now have one, and the tier table names them. api/src/memvault/\nsits at tier 3 beside knowledge/ (imports data/ + config only). api/src/security/ is 2b - THE\nvalue-keyed redaction module plus origin-binding and path-containment; it imports services/ and is\nconsumed by cofre/, bridge/, integrations/ and automation/. api/src/cofre/ is 2c - credential\ncustody whose raw item/grant stores are lint-banned outside the module, so every read goes through\nunwrap(); it imports data/ + security/ and is consumed by bridge/, automation/ and routes/. They are\nnumbered 2b/2c rather than renumbering the table because they genuinely sit between the existing\ntiers 2 and 3, and every other document's tier reference stays valid.\n\nThe merge also moved ONE edge on this map: automation/service.ts no longer re-derives the approval\nscope for a local_command consent. executors/local-command.ts records the owner+org+MACHINE scope it\nlooked the approval up under onto the run's ConsentRequest, and resolveConsent banks the answer in\nthat scope. Before this the executor read under the connected daemon's real pairingId while the\nresolver wrote under null, so \"aprovar sempre\" stored a row the lookup could never read and the\nprompt returned forever. The party that will RE-READ the key is now the party that decides it.",
   "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-31 (merge of the capability-contract and Cofre lines): three modules that had\nshipped WITHOUT a place on this map now have one, and the tier table names them. api/src/memvault/\nsits at tier 3 beside knowledge/ (imports data/ + config only). api/src/security/ is 2b - THE\nvalue-keyed redaction module plus origin-binding and path-containment; it imports services/ and is\nconsumed by cofre/, bridge/, integrations/ and automation/. api/src/cofre/ is 2c - credential\ncustody whose raw item/grant stores are lint-banned outside the module, so every read goes through\nunwrap(); it imports data/ + security/ and is consumed by bridge/, automation/ and routes/. They are\nnumbered 2b/2c rather than renumbering the table because they genuinely sit between the existing\ntiers 2 and 3, and every other document's tier reference stays valid.\n\nThe merge also moved ONE edge on this map: automation/service.ts no longer re-derives the approval\nscope for a local_command consent. executors/local-command.ts records the owner+org+MACHINE scope it\nlooked the approval up under onto the run's ConsentRequest, and resolveConsent banks the answer in\nthat scope. Before this the executor read under the connected daemon's real pairingId while the\nresolver wrote under null, so \"aprovar sempre\" stored a row the lookup could never read and the\nprompt returned forever. The party that will RE-READ the key is now the party that decides it.",
   "fontSize": 14,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "strokeColor": "#b45309",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
