   "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (c) - P2 RE-CUT, VERIFICATION FIXES. AMENDS the (b) note above: (b)'s module map\nstill stands, what changes here is the REPLAY's execution and two gate decisions it got wrong.\n\n1. THE IN-PAGE REPLAY NOW ACTUALLY RUNS IN THE AUTHENTICATED SESSION.\n   (b) shipped a replay that ran its fetch on whatever page the lease held - and a lease taken for a\n   replay holds a fresh about:blank in a profile whose jar was wiped at the previous run's release.\n   A fetch from about:blank is a CROSS-SITE request from an opaque origin: SameSite cookies are not\n   attached, a credentialed cross-origin read needs a CORS echo no private API sends, and Origin is\n   null. It inherited NOTHING - a slower Node fetch wearing a browser costume.\n   browser/inject.ts ensureOriginForCall now puts the page on the CALL'S OWN ORIGIN first (the origin\n   ROOT, never the call URL - navigating to the URL would issue the call as a document navigation),\n   and REFUSES if it could not get there or was redirected off it.\n\n2. THE LEARNED HEADER NAMES ARE FORWARDED, WHICH THEY WERE NOT.\n   The daemon resolved values from recorders.get(leaseId) and forwarded {} when there was none - and\n   on a replay lease there is never one, because a replay run does not drive the automation that\n   arms capture. recipe.headerNames, the single most valuable thing a capture learns, was dropped on\n   the one path that exists to use it. runInjectedCallOp now arms ensureRecorder(buffer:false) BEFORE\n   the navigation, so the site's own boot traffic reveals the CURRENT value of each learned name.\n   buffer:false is not a detail: nothing drains a replay's recorder, so buffering there would\n   accumulate response bodies no code can ever read.\n\n3. A mutates:false ACTION THAT LEARNS A POST IS NO LONGER BRICKED.\n   (b) answered awaiting_consent, naming a consent nobody could give: at the automation seam\n   writeAssent is false ONLY for an action declared mutates:false, and such an action is never put\n   to a human at all. putRecipe will not overwrite and supersedeRecipe only bumps, so the action\n   failed on the same gate forever with no control its owner could touch. A read-declared action\n   learning a POST is ORDINARY (portals serve searches over POST), so the RECIPE gives way:\n   runAutomationForAction clears it (recipe-store.clearRecipe, NEW) and runs the authored steps.\n\n4. AN ACTION'S ASSENT NEVER AUTHORISES A COMPILED CALL SET.\n   learnFromRun refuses to STORE any recipe containing a write, by either route, and the heal no\n   longer inherits input.writeAssent. Approving \"this action may write\" is not approving \"issue\n   these four POSTs to these four URLs\"; nothing in this slice shows a human a compiled call set,\n   so nothing may claim they approved one.\n\n5. THE RAW EVIDENCE ENDS ITS LIFE. capture -> learn -> compile -> DISCARD: discardCapture had no\n   production caller, so every learn wrote a new captureId and none was ever removed. learnFromRun\n   now discards the evidence behind the recipe it just replaced, once the new one is live.\n\n6. POSTURE IS RESOLVED FOR EVERY CALL ON EVERY RUNG and recorded on InjectedCallResolved.posture.\n   It was consulted only on the way to node-http, so a replay holding a browser session classified\n   nothing at all. in-page stays deliberately NOT posture-refusable - it is the rung an adversarial\n   origin requires - and what bounds it is PROVENANCE: every origin in a recipe was compiled from\n   traffic the site's own page generated.\n\nWHERE THE PROOF LIVES, since api/ may not import clients/ (lint-enforced) and therefore CANNOT\nexercise the daemon: clients/bridge/test/browser/inject-inheritance.test.ts drives the production\nrunInjectedCall over a REAL Chromium against a REAL server and asserts on the headers the SERVER\nreceived - the SameSite=Strict cookie and the live header value - each against its own control.\nreplay-wiring.test.ts drives the real frame handler for a lease that never captured. The api\nacceptance suite now says plainly that its daemon is a stand-in, and pins the hosted half only.",
   "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (c) - P2 RE-CUT, VERIFICATION FIXES. AMENDS the (b) note above: (b)'s module map\nstill stands, what changes here is the REPLAY's execution and two gate decisions it got wrong.\n\n1. THE IN-PAGE REPLAY NOW ACTUALLY RUNS IN THE AUTHENTICATED SESSION.\n   (b) shipped a replay that ran its fetch on whatever page the lease held - and a lease taken for a\n   replay holds a fresh about:blank in a profile whose jar was wiped at the previous run's release.\n   A fetch from about:blank is a CROSS-SITE request from an opaque origin: SameSite cookies are not\n   attached, a credentialed cross-origin read needs a CORS echo no private API sends, and Origin is\n   null. It inherited NOTHING - a slower Node fetch wearing a browser costume.\n   browser/inject.ts ensureOriginForCall now puts the page on the CALL'S OWN ORIGIN first (the origin\n   ROOT, never the call URL - navigating to the URL would issue the call as a document navigation),\n   and REFUSES if it could not get there or was redirected off it.\n\n2. THE LEARNED HEADER NAMES ARE FORWARDED, WHICH THEY WERE NOT.\n   The daemon resolved values from recorders.get(leaseId) and forwarded {} when there was none - and\n   on a replay lease there is never one, because a replay run does not drive the automation that\n   arms capture. recipe.headerNames, the single most valuable thing a capture learns, was dropped on\n   the one path that exists to use it. runInjectedCallOp now arms ensureRecorder(buffer:false) BEFORE\n   the navigation, so the site's own boot traffic reveals the CURRENT value of each learned name.\n   buffer:false is not a detail: nothing drains a replay's recorder, so buffering there would\n   accumulate response bodies no code can ever read.\n\n3. A mutates:false ACTION THAT LEARNS A POST IS NO LONGER BRICKED.\n   (b) answered awaiting_consent, naming a consent nobody could give: at the automation seam\n   writeAssent is false ONLY for an action declared mutates:false, and such an action is never put\n   to a human at all. putRecipe will not overwrite and supersedeRecipe only bumps, so the action\n   failed on the same gate forever with no control its owner could touch. A read-declared action\n   learning a POST is ORDINARY (portals serve searches over POST), so the RECIPE gives way:\n   runAutomationForAction clears it (recipe-store.clearRecipe, NEW) and runs the authored steps.\n\n4. AN ACTION'S ASSENT NEVER AUTHORISES A COMPILED CALL SET.\n   learnFromRun refuses to STORE any recipe containing a write, by either route, and the heal no\n   longer inherits input.writeAssent. Approving \"this action may write\" is not approving \"issue\n   these four POSTs to these four URLs\"; nothing in this slice shows a human a compiled call set,\n   so nothing may claim they approved one.\n\n5. THE RAW EVIDENCE ENDS ITS LIFE. capture -> learn -> compile -> DISCARD: discardCapture had no\n   production caller, so every learn wrote a new captureId and none was ever removed. learnFromRun\n   now discards the evidence behind the recipe it just replaced, once the new one is live.\n\n6. POSTURE IS RESOLVED FOR EVERY CALL ON EVERY RUNG and recorded on InjectedCallResolved.posture.\n   It was consulted only on the way to node-http, so a replay holding a browser session classified\n   nothing at all. in-page stays deliberately NOT posture-refusable - it is the rung an adversarial\n   origin requires - and what bounds it is PROVENANCE: every origin in a recipe was compiled from\n   traffic the site's own page generated.\n\nWHERE THE PROOF LIVES, since api/ may not import clients/ (lint-enforced) and therefore CANNOT\nexercise the daemon: clients/bridge/test/browser/inject-inheritance.test.ts drives the production\nrunInjectedCall over a REAL Chromium against a REAL server and asserts on the headers the SERVER\nreceived - the SameSite=Strict cookie and the live header value - each against its own control.\nreplay-wiring.test.ts drives the real frame handler for a lease that never captured. The api\nacceptance suite now says plainly that its daemon is a stand-in, and pins the hosted half only.",
