   "fontFamily": 3,
   "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (e) - P2 ROUND FIVE: THE THREE REMAINING COMPILATION-SOUNDNESS HOLES. AMENDS (d).\nNo module, seam or stored shape changes here. What changes is WHEN two existing decisions are taken and\nHOW ONE EXISTING FIELD IS READ - and each of the three was a way of answering confidently wrong.\n\n1. THE RECIPE'S HOLES AND THE RUN'S ARGUMENTS ARE THE SAME SET, PROVED BOTH WAYS.\n   (d) item 3 refused, at COMPILE time, to learn a recipe that ignores an argument. At REPLAY the same\n   shape was a shrug: an argument no template had a hole for was dropped, the call went out, and the\n   caller got the data the recipe was compiled around with outcome `ok`. Reachable from a recipe an\n   older build stored and from any caller that starts passing a new argument.\n     injected-call.assertHolesSupplied          args >= holes  - a hole nobody filled widens the query\n     injected-call.assertEveryArgumentHasAHole  holes >= args  - an argument nothing can carry   [NEW]\n   Asked of the RECIPE, not of one call: a flow's second hop routinely takes no argument. Exemptions\n   are the compile's own and read through the compile's own SECRET_SHAPED_INPUT_NAME (a secret-shaped\n   NAME is never holed and never required; a null/undefined argument carries nothing). A NON-SCALAR\n   argument is refused, not exempted. The refusal is a FALL-THROUGH (`no-recipe`), like its mirror:\n   the authored steps see every argument, so it costs the optimisation and never the answer.\n\n2. `mutates` HAS ONE READING IN THIS REPO AND IT IS `!== false`.\n   action-consent.actionRequiresConsent owns it: the field arrives off an unvalidated config.json and\n   off agent-authored Mongo rows, so absent / \"false\" / 0 / null are all WRITES. The spine restated it\n   as `=== true` in three places, inverting the rule for exactly those values - and the consequence is\n   (d) item 1 re-opened from the other side.\n     action-executor.ts   now CALLS actionRequiresConsent(action) instead of restating it.\n     service.ts           normalises the optional seam field ONCE: mutating = input.mutates !== false\n     replay-action.ts     ReplayActionInput.mutates : boolean   (REQUIRED past the seam)\n     injected-call.ts     ReplayInput.mutates       : boolean   (REQUIRED past the seam)\n   Optional at the seam (Rule 7: an added field may not change an existing implementer), definite\n   inside it, so a caller that forgets is a compile error rather than a silently mutating action.\n\n3. STORABILITY IS DECIDED BEFORE THE RECORDER IS ARMED.\n   A mutating action stores no recipe at all (the two refusals of (d) item 1), and the pass was armed\n   for it anyway. While armed, the machine's NetworkRecorder holds the live VALUE of every header the\n   authenticated page sends (clients/bridge/src/browser/capture.ts, the `live` map) - so arming for a\n   run that cannot learn extends a credential's residency on the user's machine and ships a full\n   pass's bodies over the wire, to reach a refusal decidable before the run began.\n     runAutomationForAction:\n       named    = integrationKey && actionName   -> the REPLAY is tried (an old recipe must be seen\n                                                    to be cleared)\n       storable = named && !mutating             -> RunAutomationOptions.observeNetwork is passed\n                                                    ONLY here, and the engine arms iff it is\n   The duplicate mutates check inside learnFromRun was REMOVED, not kept as a second line: nothing\n   reaches it now, and an unreachable gate is one a reviewer trusts and a mutation cannot kill.\n\nALSO: redactCaptures wrapped redactStream (which IS registry-then-name) in a second redactBodyByName.\nIdempotent, so nothing was wrong in effect - but every captured body was parsed and re-serialised\ntwice on the capture-persist path, and the doubling made one leg read as two. The wrap is gone and the\nleg is pinned for both bodies.",
   "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (e) - P2 ROUND FIVE: THE THREE REMAINING COMPILATION-SOUNDNESS HOLES. AMENDS (d).\nNo module, seam or stored shape changes here. What changes is WHEN two existing decisions are taken and\nHOW ONE EXISTING FIELD IS READ - and each of the three was a way of answering confidently wrong.\n\n1. THE RECIPE'S HOLES AND THE RUN'S ARGUMENTS ARE THE SAME SET, PROVED BOTH WAYS.\n   (d) item 3 refused, at COMPILE time, to learn a recipe that ignores an argument. At REPLAY the same\n   shape was a shrug: an argument no template had a hole for was dropped, the call went out, and the\n   caller got the data the recipe was compiled around with outcome `ok`. Reachable from a recipe an\n   older build stored and from any caller that starts passing a new argument.\n     injected-call.assertHolesSupplied          args >= holes  - a hole nobody filled widens the query\n     injected-call.assertEveryArgumentHasAHole  holes >= args  - an argument nothing can carry   [NEW]\n   Asked of the RECIPE, not of one call: a flow's second hop routinely takes no argument. Exemptions\n   are the compile's own and read through the compile's own SECRET_SHAPED_INPUT_NAME (a secret-shaped\n   NAME is never holed and never required; a null/undefined argument carries nothing). A NON-SCALAR\n   argument is refused, not exempted. The refusal is a FALL-THROUGH (`no-recipe`), like its mirror:\n   the authored steps see every argument, so it costs the optimisation and never the answer.\n\n2. `mutates` HAS ONE READING IN THIS REPO AND IT IS `!== false`.\n   action-consent.actionRequiresConsent owns it: the field arrives off an unvalidated config.json and\n   off agent-authored Mongo rows, so absent / \"false\" / 0 / null are all WRITES. The spine restated it\n   as `=== true` in three places, inverting the rule for exactly those values - and the consequence is\n   (d) item 1 re-opened from the other side.\n     action-executor.ts   now CALLS actionRequiresConsent(action) instead of restating it.\n     service.ts           normalises the optional seam field ONCE: mutating = input.mutates !== false\n     replay-action.ts     ReplayActionInput.mutates : boolean   (REQUIRED past the seam)\n     injected-call.ts     ReplayInput.mutates       : boolean   (REQUIRED past the seam)\n   Optional at the seam (Rule 7: an added field may not change an existing implementer), definite\n   inside it, so a caller that forgets is a compile error rather than a silently mutating action.\n\n3. STORABILITY IS DECIDED BEFORE THE RECORDER IS ARMED.\n   A mutating action stores no recipe at all (the two refusals of (d) item 1), and the pass was armed\n   for it anyway. While armed, the machine's NetworkRecorder holds the live VALUE of every header the\n   authenticated page sends (clients/bridge/src/browser/capture.ts, the `live` map) - so arming for a\n   run that cannot learn extends a credential's residency on the user's machine and ships a full\n   pass's bodies over the wire, to reach a refusal decidable before the run began.\n     runAutomationForAction:\n       named    = integrationKey && actionName   -> the REPLAY is tried (an old recipe must be seen\n                                                    to be cleared)\n       storable = named && !mutating             -> RunAutomationOptions.observeNetwork is passed\n                                                    ONLY here, and the engine arms iff it is\n   The duplicate mutates check inside learnFromRun was REMOVED, not kept as a second line: nothing\n   reaches it now, and an unreachable gate is one a reviewer trusts and a mutation cannot kill.\n\nALSO: redactCaptures wrapped redactStream (which IS registry-then-name) in a second redactBodyByName.\nIdempotent, so nothing was wrong in effect - but every captured body was parsed and re-serialised\ntwice on the capture-persist path, and the doubling made one leg read as two. The wrap is gone and the\nleg is pinned for both bodies.",
